Darwin Core is the shared standard used by natural history museums and portals
such as GBIF. Turning a paper catalog into Darwin Core normally means retyping
everything into a spreadsheet and manually fixing spellings, units, dates, and
missing localities. Because you resolved those issues at entry, NAHPU can build
the archive directly from your linked records.

In this final tutorial, you will review the seven specimens, explore their
summary statistics, export a Darwin Core Archive, and inspect the output.

## 1. Review before exporting

Check your records against the paper catalog one last time. Tutorials 3–5
prevented most errors at entry; this pass catches anything left over.

| Check | Where | What to verify |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Field IDs | Specimens list | `HH436`, `HH437`, `HH442`, `HH453`, `HH456`, `HH523`, `HH529`, each listed once |
| Taxon names | Each specimen | One spelling per name; *Maxomys* (genus) only on `HH523` |
| Localities | Each specimen | Pondok Merah for `HH436–456`; Pasir Putih for `HH523` and `HH529` |
| Methods | Each specimen | `Pitfall` for `HH453` and `HH529`; `Snap trap` for the rest |
| Documented decisions | `Remarks` | `HH437`, `HH442`, `HH456`, `HH523`, and `HH529` note what changed and why |
| Gaps | Sites and events | No coordinates for Pasir Putih; no uncertainty for Pondok Merah; no pitfall count for Line 4 |

Do not "fix" those gaps before exporting—they are honest records of what the
2014 paper catalog did not capture.

{% aside type="tip" %}
For a quick spreadsheet view to proofread, use `Export records` with Darwin
Core headers. See
[Export tabular records](/en/how-to-recipes/export-and-print/export-tabular-records/).
{% /aside %}

## 2. Explore the statistics

Before exporting, look at the dataset as a whole. Reviewing record statistics
is a core NAHPU best practice: it highlights sampling imbalances, tracks how
consistently specimen parts were collected, and—during active fieldwork—helps
you prioritize parts when supplies such as tubes or ethanol run low.

On the dashboard, open `Record statistics` and select `Explore more stats`. The
`Summary` view totals the project:

![NAHPU Record statistics summary on an iPad: 7 specimens, 19 specimen parts, 5 narratives, 7 recorded sites, 6 events, and a taxonomic breakdown of 2 orders, 3 families, 4 genera, and 5 species](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/statistics-summary.webp)

### Check for sampling imbalance

Select `Explore` > `Counts` > `Specimens`. Group by `Species`, break down by
`Sex`, and switch to `Table`:

![NAHPU Explore view on an iPad showing specimens by species and sex as a table](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/statistics-species-sex.webp)

Five of the seven specimens are male, and *Maxomys hylomyoides* is the only
species represented by both sexes. Seven hand-picked records are too small to
draw conclusions, so treat this as a demonstration. With a full catalog, the
same table reveals whether a skew reflects trap bias or seasonality and should
be noted before analysis.

### Check part sampling

Select `Part quantity` and group by `Part type`:

![NAHPU Explore view on an iPad showing part quantity by part type: Liver 6, Whole body 4, Skeleton 3, Skin 3, Skull 3](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/statistics-parts.webp)

Six of the seven specimens include a liver sample; the lone exception is
`HH442`, whose paper form has no tissue circled. In the field, checking this
table at the end of each day shows whether every specimen received the parts
your protocol requires and which part types consume the most tubes, ethanol,
and formalin.

Every table here includes an `Export table` button for reports. See
[Export statistics](/en/usages/export/export-statistics/).

### Scan records in the Data table

To inspect individual rows, open `Data table` under `Tools` at the bottom of
the dashboard. Pick a view—`Specimen records`, `Specimen parts`, `Sites`,
`Events`, or `Narrative`—then sort, search, or filter. Below is the parts table
for the seven specimens:

![NAHPU Data table on an iPad listing specimen parts with type, count, treatment, cataloger, and field number](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/data-table-parts.webp)

The Data table lets you spot gaps—such as a part missing its treatment or a
specimen missing a tissue—without opening each record one by one.

## 3. Create the archive

{% steps %}

1. From the `Dashboard`, open the project menu and select `Bundle records`.
2. Choose `Darwin Core Archive` as `Bundle format` (always packaged as ZIP).
3. Select `Selected taxa` and choose `Mammals`.
4. Name the file `mt-tujuh-2014` and choose a destination folder.
5. Inspect `Package contents`. Expand `occurrence.csv` to review its columns;
   this view also flags any recorded fields omitted because they lack a Darwin
   Core term.
6. Read any warnings, then select `Create bundle`.

{% /steps %}

NAHPU writes `mt-tujuh-2014.dwca.zip`.

## 4. Inspect the archive

Unzip `mt-tujuh-2014.dwca.zip` to see its structure:

{% filetree %}
- meta.xml
- eml.xml
- occurrence.csv
- material.csv
- measurement_or_fact.csv
{% /filetree %}

`occurrence.csv` is the core table, with one row per specimen. `material.csv`
and `measurement_or_fact.csv` store parts and measurements linked back to each
specimen, `meta.xml` describes the schema, and `eml.xml` stores the dataset
metadata.

### One specimen, one row

Below is part of the `HH436` row (your UUID and Event ID will differ):

| Darwin Core term | Value | Source in NAHPU |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `occurrenceID` | A UUID | Generated by NAHPU when the record was created |
| `basisOfRecord` | `PreservedSpecimen` | Specimen record |
| `catalogNumber` | `HH436` | Field ID |
| `eventID` | The `TJHH-L1` event | Collecting event |
| `eventDate`, `eventTime` | `2014-09-12`, `16:00` | Specimen |
| `scientificName` | `Lariscus insignis` | Taxon registry |
| `family` | `Sciuridae` | Taxon registry |
| `country`, `stateProvince`, `county` | `Indonesia`, `Jambi`, `Kerinci` | Site |
| `locality` | `Gunung Tujuh, Sungai Jernih, Pondok Merah, beside Lake Gunung Tujuh` | Site |
| `decimalLatitude`, `decimalLongitude` | `-1.70274`, `101.39032` | Site coordinate |
| `geodeticDatum` | `WGS84` | Site coordinate |
| `minimumElevationInMeters` | `2004` | Site coordinate |
| `habitat` | Type, condition, and description | Site |
| `recordedBy` | `Heru Handika` | Event personnel |
| `identifiedBy` | `Heru Handika` | Specimen |
| `sex`, `lifeStage` | `Male`, `Subadult` | Specimen |

On paper, these values were scattered across sheet headers, the field journal,
and the collector's memory. Here, each value comes cleanly from the record that
owns it.

### How the paper problems resolved

| Paper catalog | Darwin Core Archive |
| --- | --- |
| Five spellings of *Maxomys hylomyoides* | One consistent `scientificName` value |
| `01.70274° S`, crossed-out `feet`, no datum | `-1.70274`, elevation in meters, explicit `WGS84` datum (with missing uncertainty left blank) |
| Blank locality headers | Every row carries its full locality |
| Pasir Putih filed under Pondok Merah | `HH523` and `HH529` carry the Pasir Putih locality with no coordinates |
| `Line 1` covering two methods | Pitfall and snap-trap captures link to separate events |
| Heru listed as collector on every sheet | `recordedBy` names the person from the event |
| Undocumented `SA` and `AL` codes | Rows in `material.csv` with explicit type and treatment (`Skull`, `Whole body` in `Formalin`, `Liver` in `ETOH`) |
| `TTL 238`, `TL 84`, … with no units | Rows in `measurement_or_fact.csv` with names and units (`total length`, `238`, `mm`) |

{% aside type="caution" title="Validate before publishing" %}
Before submitting an archive to a museum or portal, run it through a validator
such as the [GBIF data validator](https://www.gbif.org/tools/data-validator).
Fix any reported issues inside NAHPU—not by hand-editing the CSV files—then
export again.
{% /aside %}

## What you accomplished

Across these seven tutorials, you:

- audited a real paper catalog and identified its data-quality risks;
- created a project with personnel records and a standardized taxon registry;
- converted one locality header into a camp and six line sites, and journal
  notes into six collecting events;
- entered seven specimens, documenting every correction and inference;
- cleaned up and entered the field journal linked to the camp where it was
  written;
- reviewed sample balance and part coverage using record statistics and the
  Data table;
- exported a standards-compliant Darwin Core Archive directly from linked
  records.

Young Heru's paper catalog was already more thorough than many: it included
standard measurements, trap lines, and a field journal. NAHPU does not replace
that field discipline—it preserves the links between place, time, method,
people, and specimen parts so none of that context disappears when the notebook
closes.

